<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The one process everything else depends on, and the only one nothing was watching.

         On 2026-11-08 OpenD exited cleanly after 23 days up, nothing restarted it, and the sweep
         failed 646 times over 2h42m. The market was shut, so it cost nothing — which was luck
         rather than design. Both optionality agents are managed by launchd and the thing they
         both depend on was a hand-started desktop app.

         A TIMER rather than KeepAlive, which is the whole design decision here. KeepAlive watches
         the process it launched, and `openD.sh` starts OpenD in the background and returns — so
         launchd would see the script finish a second later, conclude the job ended, and run it
         again. Immediately. For ever.

         More importantly, KeepAlive can only see whether a process EXISTS. An OpenD sitting at a
         login prompt with its websocket port shut looks perfectly healthy to the operating
         system, and that is the failure that matters: the service would show dashes while launchd
         reported everything fine. `openD.sh ensure` waits for the API port and pings the telnet
         console to confirm there is an upstream session, so it can tell the two apart. The script
         is the part that knows something; this agent is just a clock.

         A minute's granularity against the 2h42m it replaces.

         It runs the COMMITTED copy, `deploy/macos/opend.sh`, not ~/openD.sh — so the version-controlled
         one is the one that actually runs, and drift shows up as behaviour rather than only as a
         failing checksum. ~/openD.sh stays as the copy that works with no repository cloned. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.optionality.opend</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__REPO__/deploy/macos/opend.sh</string>
        <!-- idempotent and quiet when all is well: starts a dead OpenD, restarts a husk whose API
             port is shut, and only REPORTS one that is up without a confirmed session — because
             that may need a human at the login window, and a restart a minute against an expired
             credential is a watchdog flapping a provider. -->
        <string>ensure</string>
    </array>
    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__HOME__</string>
    <!-- SCRIPT_DIR in the script resolves OPEND_PATH, and it must still find ~/_workspace/OpenD
         when the script itself lives in the repo — so the working directory stays home. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- launchd starts with a bare PATH, and `ensure` reaches for python3, nc and pgrep -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <!-- stamped from .env at install time, which is why the committed script carries no
             account and this generated file (outside the repo) does -->
        <key>OPEND_LOGIN_ACCOUNT</key>
        <string>__OPEND_ACCOUNT__</string>
        <!-- the script derives OPEND_PATH from its own location by default; the committed copy
             lives in the repo, so it is told where OpenD actually is -->
        <key>OPEND_PATH</key>
        <string>__HOME__/_workspace/OpenD</string>
    </dict>
    <!-- WITHOUT THIS, THE WATCHDOG KILLS WHAT IT STARTS.
         `opend.sh start` launches OpenD in the background and returns, and when a launchd job's
         own process exits launchd tears down the rest of its process group — taking OpenD with
         it. nohup protects against SIGHUP, not against that. The result was a clean OpenD exit
         about 27 seconds after every tick and a restart on the next one: 39 starts in an hour,
         each one looking successful in the log.

         It passed three "kill it and watch it recover" demonstrations because each check came
         seconds after the restart, before the teardown. The lesson is in the test, not the key:
         a process-supervision change is verified by watching it STAY up, not by watching it come
         up. -->
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- at login, and then every minute -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/optionality-opend.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/optionality-opend.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
